```text
==============================================================
=/                     L I B V P O R T                      \=
==============================================================

       Reference client library for the VPort protocol


  VPort is a tiny display protocol for embedded and
  point-of-sale Linux systems. A client talks to a display
  server over a Unix socket and the server draws in an
  isolated portal. libvport is the reference client library.

  STATUS: early release. The protocol is a draft and can
  change in any 0.x version. libvport does not speak TLS yet,
  so it only talks to a server on the same machine through
  the Unix socket.

  The specification and the reference server (vportd) are in
  the VPort repository linked from this crate.


--[ EXAMPLE ]-------------------------------------------------


  use libvport::{Connection, Event};

  fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
      let mut conn = Connection::connect_default()?;
      conn.init_portal(1, 100, 100, 640, 360)?;
      conn.draw_rect(1, 0, 0, 640, 360, 0x202020ff)?;
      conn.show_text(1, 20, 20, 48, 0xffffffff, "Hello")?;
      conn.commit(1)?;
      loop {
          if let Event::PointerButton(_) = conn.wait_event()? {
              return Ok(());
          }
      }
  }

  connect_default reads the socket path from VPORT_SOCKET and
  falls back to /run/vport/vport.sock.


==============================================================
```
